    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i look at the firewall and network rules and everything seems fine. the timeouts are so sporadic, it's almost like they're appearing out of nowhere. i've even looked at the agent logs at the exact timestamp of the failure and they're super vague, just saying \"connection refused\" or \"service unavailable\" without any real lead. it's like something is interfering with the process only sometimes. do you think there's a chance it's a race condition or something with the way the nodes are being recycled? it's honestly getting so frustrating, feels like there's a ghost in the machine or something.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sporadic \"connection refused\" errors despite stable network rules strongly suggest a timing issue related to service startup or agent lifecycle management. It's highly probable that the ephemeral nature of your nodes is causing a race condition where the test runner starts before a necessary dependency or required service inside the container/agent is fully ready. Consider implementing stronger readiness probes or introducing deliberate, short delays before executing the unit tests during the pipeline.",
      "source": "target",
